<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- REQUIRED. MACVM is a JIT: it maps its code cache MAP_JIT and flips the
       thread's W^X view with pthread_jit_write_protect_np (docs/arm64.md §2,
       src/codecache/). Notarization requires the hardened runtime, and the
       hardened runtime refuses MAP_JIT without this entitlement — so without
       it the app signs, notarizes, launches, and then dies the moment a method
       tiers up. There is no fallback: JitMode::Off is not a supported
       configuration for the embedded VM. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- NOT set, deliberately — each would widen the attack surface for a
       capability MACVM does not need under Developer ID distribution:

       com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
         A blunter instrument than allow-jit; MAP_JIT is the sanctioned path
         and is sufficient.

       com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
         Would be needed only if the FFI tier dlopen'd unsigned third-party
         dylibs. Tier 1 resolves through dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT) against
         already-loaded system images, which library validation permits.
         Add it only if loading foreign dylibs becomes a supported feature,
         and document why at that point.

       com.apple.security.app-sandbox
         Mac App Store only. Under Developer ID it would break the FFI tier
         (guest syscalls hit kernel-level denials) for no distribution
         benefit. See the App Store notes in tools/make-macapp.sh.

       com.apple.security.network.server
         The control channel (MACVM_COCOA_CTL) is off unless that variable is
         set, and is a development affordance. If it is ever enabled by
         default, this entitlement AND a review of the eval surface are
         prerequisites. -->
</dict>
</plist>
